WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:06.080
Dustin Hoffman once tried to keep things real
-- really real -- by making fun of his co-star's

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.990
dead boyfriend, and slapping her in the face
-- allegedly.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.610
If that wasn't shocking enough, his co-star
was Meryl Streep.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:18.609
Naive daddy's girl Baby and macho dancer Johnny
are not immediately in sync when they first

00:00:18.609 --> 00:00:21.970
meet in the 1987 smash hit Dirty Dancing.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.699
She thinks he's rude, he thinks she's immature.

00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:29.400
This matched the tone of the relationship
between stars Grey and Swayze offscreen, too.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.650
Swayze later told the American Film Institute
that much of the film's training montage was

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:38.220
taken from sessions in which Swayze, a trained
dancer, really was trying to teach Grey the

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.290
right moves.

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:42.330
All that frustration on his face is pretty
much real.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:47.640
Grey later described their relationship on
the Dirty Dancing set as "a very complex dynamic."

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.640
It didn't help that the two had previously
spent eight weeks in a military training camp,

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.020
preparing for their roles in the World War
III movie Red Dawn.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:59.030
Grey felt Swayze took his group leader role
too seriously in that production, ordering

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:00.780
everyone else around.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.500
When she was cast in Dirty Dancing, she even
secretly hoped he wouldn't get the part of

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:05.500
Johnny.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:06.500
"I'll never be sorry."

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.140
"Neither will I."

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:14.610
However, the two ultimately found a level
of professional respect.

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:18.380
Grey appreciated that Swayze promised he would
protect her, which helped the nervous actress

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:19.680
through their big lifts.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.450
And to AFI, Swayze described Grey as "one
of the most gifted actresses around."

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:25.450
He added:

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:28.130
"That's what made [...] her character really,
really special."

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.810
Shakespeare once wrote that "the course of
true love never did run smooth" — and neither

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.690
did the relationship between the two actors
who brought one of his greatest plays to the

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:38.690
screen.

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:43.240
Claire Danes was only 17 when she appeared
in Baz Luhrmann's [BAHZ LUR-mun] 1996 Shakespeare

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.220
adaptation Romeo + Juliet ["Romeo and Juliet"].

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:48.460
Her co-star Leonardo DiCaprio, meanwhile,
was 21.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:53.490
However, Danes was said to be put off by what
she saw as DiCaprio's immaturity, particularly

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:56.229
the pranks he would pull on the cast and crew.

00:01:56.229 --> 00:01:58.960
Between takes, they totally ignored each other.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.090
DiCaprio maintained his prankster reputation
after moving on to play a now-equally famous

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.770
star-crossed lover.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:09.920
During production on Titanic, he and his co-star
Kate Winslet even bonded over pranks.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:12.460
As DiCaprio's Titanic co-star Billy Zane put
it:

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:17.720
"If he wasn't rolling back his eyelids, he
was making objets d'art out of bodily fluids."

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.960
While Winslet apparently found this hilarious,
Danes had had more than enough after the Romeo

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.290
shoot ended.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:28.060
She was even approached to star in Titanic
opposite DiCaprio, but turned the part down.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:32.420
She later claimed that she wasn't ready for
the attention such a big project would bring.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.870
There may have been more to those awkward
silences than hatred, however.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:40.700
Miriam Margoyles, who played Juliet's nurse,
has since claimed that Danes had a big crush

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:45.220
on her older co-star, who handled it insensitively
and simply tried to stay away from her.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:50.120
Although it takes a World War and a broken
engagement for the central couple in The Notebook

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:54.440
to get together for good, the sparks between
Noah and Allie are immediate.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:58.030
Unlike their characters, however, it took
years for the actors to fully warm up to each

00:02:58.030 --> 00:02:59.030
other.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:03.510
Neither Rachel McAdams nor Ryan Gosling were
the studio's first choices for their roles.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.790
Their chemistry helped them land the parts,
but it didn't guarantee an easy shoot.

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:11.360
Director Nick Cassavetes later recalled that
while they were shooting a big scene with

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:15.610
lots of extras, Gosling asked him to have
a different actress stand out of shot and

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:17.050
read McAdams' lines.

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:18.170
He allegedly said:

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:20.450
"I'm just not getting anything from this."

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:24.650
Cassavetes ultimately pushed the pair to resolve
their conflict for the sake of the shoot — by

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.530
letting them air their respective issues in
a shouting match.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:28.630
Cassavetes remembered:

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:33.440
"The rest of the film wasn't smooth sailing,
but it was smoother sailing."

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.790
The Notebook was released in summer 2004 and
became an instant classic.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:42.610
By 2005, Gosling and McAdams weren't just
on speaking terms: They were officially dating

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:43.769
for real.

00:03:43.769 --> 00:03:49.099
That year, they even won the MTV Movie Award
for Best Onscreen Kiss, for that rain-drenched

00:03:49.099 --> 00:03:50.920
moment in The Notebook.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:56.100
Gosling's and McAdams' romance ended in 2007,
however, and it sounded stormy to the bitter

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.100
end.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:58.100
Gosling told GQ:

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.520
"We both went down swingin' and we called
it a draw."

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:06.360
Despite spending much of the late '90s and
early '00s as the go-to charmingly bumbling

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:11.110
rom-com hero, there's no love lost between
Hugh Grant and most of his co-stars.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:15.250
Grant himself once freely admitted on an episode
of Watch What Happens Live:

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:19.889
"No, I'm a nasty piece of work, and I think
people should know that."

00:04:19.889 --> 00:04:23.760
Grant's self-awareness about his real-life
negative effect on women potentially peaked

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.820
in an interview with Elle in 2009.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:30.790
Asked to give three adjectives about several
of his former co-stars, he said that Julianne

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:37.010
Moore, Rachel Weisz, and Drew Barrymore loathe,
despise and hate him respectively.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:42.110
Grant added another name to that list in 2015
on Watch What Happens Live, too, saying that

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:44.820
Julia Roberts might also hate him, because:

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:48.139
"I've probably made too many jokes about the
size of her mouth."

00:04:48.139 --> 00:04:51.940
At least one person appears to have forgiven
Grant's rudeness, however.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:55.800
Although Grant made Barrymore cry on the set
of Music and Lyrics, he guested on her talk

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.600
show in 2021.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.890
During the segment, they revealed that, a
few years after the movie had come out, they

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:02.870
had accidentally made out in a New York restaurant.

00:05:04.009 --> 00:05:07.710
Seth Rogen and Katherine Heigl got on well
during the filming of their unplanned-pregnancy

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:09.180
comedy Knocked Up.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:12.570
Rogen even said on a 2016 edition of The Howard
Stern Show:

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:16.580
"As we were making the movie, honestly, I
was like, 'I would make a dozen movies with

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:17.580
her.'"

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.350
Heigl had a slightly different take, though.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:23.509
Her problem wasn't with Rogen, but with aspects
of the character she was playing: an uptight

00:05:23.509 --> 00:05:27.810
career-driven reporter who gets pregnant after
a one night stand with a slacker played by

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:28.810
Rogen.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:30.870
Heigl told Vanity Fair that she disliked that:

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:35.210
"[Knocked Up] paints the women as shrews,
as humorless and uptight, and it paints the

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:38.090
men as lovable, goofy, fun-loving guys."

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:41.300
She added that the movie was definitely "a
little sexist."

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:45.740
If this had happened today, Heigl's comments
might be praised, but in 2008, she was branded

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:47.900
as ungrateful, rude, and difficult.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:51.990
Rogen described her comments as crazy, and
said he was disappointed not to get an apology

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:54.570
— although Heigl had publicly apologized.

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:59.180
In 2016, Heigl said again that she regretted
the comments, and that she would work with

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:00.979
Rogen, if he wanted to work with her.

00:06:00.979 --> 00:06:04.990
A few months later, Rogen publicly revealed
that he no longer held a grudge against her.

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:10.050
When Hollywood newcomer Sean Young was cast
in the sci-fi epic Blade Runner, she hoped

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:13.320
that her co-star Harrison Ford would offer
her some support.

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:17.460
After all, Ford was already a household name,
thanks to his roles in Star Wars and Raiders

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:18.510
of the Lost Ark.

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:21.949
Young was especially looking for guidance
during the pair's love scene.

00:06:21.949 --> 00:06:23.400
However, she later recalled:

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:26.070
"Harrison wasn't particularly generous that
way."

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:31.070
The scene between Young's replicant Rachael
and Ford's replicant-hunter Rick Deckard was

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:34.340
so violent that the crew referred to it as
"the hate scene."

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:37.760
It wasn't just Ford that Young took issue
with on the Blade Runner set.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:42.020
She later told the Daily Beast that director
Ridley Scott deliberately shot their sex scene

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:46.360
in a way that heightened Young's discomfort,
because she'd rejected his advances.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:47.360
She said:

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:53.160
"I think it was Ridley's none-too-subtle message
that he was getting even with me.”

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:04.600
Imagine landing your first lead role in a
romantic comedy, opposite one of the most

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:06.560
famous actresses of the moment.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:10.620
Then you get to set and find out — from
her — that she didn't want you to get the

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:11.620
job.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:15.539
Jay Mohr lived that wonderful scenario in
real life, when he was cast alongside Jennifer

00:07:15.539 --> 00:07:18.720
Aniston in 1997's Picture Perfect.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:23.120
On an episode of his podcast Mohr Stories,
Mohr recalled that the first scene he and

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:26.389
Aniston had to shoot together involved them
making out.

00:07:26.389 --> 00:07:30.210
His first encounter with Aniston that day
was when she walked past him with director

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:32.080
Glenn Gordon Caron.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:33.950
According to Mohr, Aniston said to Caron:

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:39.280
"'Six guys, they screentest,' and then she
points at me and goes, 'the one fucking guy

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:40.310
I hate!'"

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:43.340
Of course, the issue may have been that one
of those other guys was Aniston's then-boyfriend,

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:45.240
Tate Donovan.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:48.000
Mohr said that this animosity continued for
the rest of the shoot.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.139
He later told Elle that Aniston would audibly
complain about him to the other actors in

00:07:52.139 --> 00:07:53.280
between takes.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:54.280
He said:

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:57.740
"Jennifer Aniston was so f---ing mean to me
that [...] I drove to New Jersey [...] I put

00:07:57.740 --> 00:07:59.760
my head in my mother's lap, and I f---ing
cried."

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:04.539
Just because you're playing soon-to-be exes
doesn't mean you have to treat your co-star

00:08:04.539 --> 00:08:06.530
with ex-level hostility.

00:08:06.530 --> 00:08:10.550
Dustin Hoffman took his notorious character
immersion too far, however, while filming

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:12.930
the divorce drama Kramer vs. Kramer.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:17.440
On the movie's shoot, Hoffman used real-life
bullying tactics to try to intimidate Meryl

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:19.789
Streep, who was playing his onscreen ex-wife.

00:08:19.789 --> 00:08:24.130
Hoffman and Streep got off to a bad start
when they first met at her audition for a

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:26.290
Broadway play he was directing.

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:30.039
Whatever exactly happened, Hoffman apologized
and Streep accepted it.

00:08:30.039 --> 00:08:33.430
But the real trouble started when the two
made Kramer vs. Kramer.

00:08:33.430 --> 00:08:38.240
Hoffman repeatedly made comments about Streep's
boyfriend John Cazale, who had recently died

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:39.399
from lung cancer.

00:08:39.399 --> 00:08:42.679
He shouted at her when she requested changes
to the script.

00:08:42.679 --> 00:08:46.190
And, in a scene in which the couple is fighting
in an Italian restaurant, he shocked Streep

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:48.820
by suddenly throwing a wine glass against
the wall.

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:53.350
In another fight scene, he spontaneously slapped
her across the face, leaving red marks.

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:56.989
Hoffman has acknowledged that he was venting
emotions brought about by the real-life divorce

00:08:56.989 --> 00:08:59.199
he was going through during the shoot.

00:08:59.199 --> 00:09:04.149
This weak explanation wasn't helped, however,
when, in 2017, multiple women accused him

00:09:04.149 --> 00:09:05.490
of sexual assault and harassment.

00:09:06.559 --> 00:09:10.689
In Breakfast at Tiffany's, the relationship
between the two metaphorical drifters Holly

00:09:10.689 --> 00:09:15.929
Golightly and Paul Varjak sparkles like the
diamonds inside that iconic jewelry store.

00:09:15.929 --> 00:09:20.179
But offscreen, Audrey Hepburn found George
Peppard "pompous," according to the movie's

00:09:20.179 --> 00:09:21.750
co-producer Richard Shepherd.

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:22.750
He recalled:

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:27.620
"There wasn't a human being that Audrey Hepburn
didn't have a kind word for, except George

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:28.860
Peppard."

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:33.690
For his part, Peppard dismissed Hepburn as
uptight and prudish, calling her "the Happy

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:34.690
Nun."

00:09:34.690 --> 00:09:39.689
As the Breakfast at Tiffany's shoot continued,
Peppard constantly tried to make his character,

00:09:39.689 --> 00:09:41.949
not Hepburn's, the center of the movie.

00:09:41.949 --> 00:09:46.550
He refused to take directions that he thought
made Paul unlikeable, and almost came to blows

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:47.850
with director Blake Edwards.

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:53.420
Renowned actor and director Laurence Olivier
was initially excited to work with the superstar

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:57.509
Marilyn Monroe on 1957's The Prince and the
Showgirl.

00:09:57.509 --> 00:10:01.470
He would be directing and starring opposite
the actress, who was fast becoming the most

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:03.440
in-demand woman in Hollywood.

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:05.529
And he was initially charmed by her too.

00:10:05.529 --> 00:10:06.550
Olivier later recalled:

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:10.739
"When I first met her, I thought she was the
most enchanting thing I'd ever met in my life

00:10:10.739 --> 00:10:15.389
[...] I thought she was such a witty actress,
as well as being so ravishingly lovely."

00:10:15.389 --> 00:10:19.809
Monroe certainly had a remarkable quality
on screen: not just in the way she looked,

00:10:19.809 --> 00:10:21.800
but in her comic timing, too.

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:25.700
Directors were keen to use that magic and
the star power it afforded her to boost their

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:26.700
projects.

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:30.470
But the price they paid was putting up with
Monroe's maddening habits.

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:34.160
She was often late to set, for example, which
would hold up the shoot.

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:39.509
And whereas Olivier was trying to make a straightforward
comedy, Monroe, following her training, insisted

00:10:39.509 --> 00:10:42.589
on being given a more intellectual meaning
for each line.

00:10:42.589 --> 00:10:45.199
Monroe didn't care for her co-star and director,
either.

00:10:45.199 --> 00:10:47.130
She even blamed him for her lateness, saying:

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:51.179
"If you don't respect your artists, they can't
work well.

00:10:51.179 --> 00:10:53.309
Respect is what you have to fight for."

